# Small Files and Functions

<version>1.0.0</version>

## Context
- Apply when writing or refactoring code in any language
- Helps maintain code readability and simplifies debugging
- Reduces cognitive load when understanding code components
- Makes code easier to test and maintain

## Requirements
- Limit file size to less than 300 lines of code (excluding comments and blank lines)
- Limit function bodies to less than 70 lines of code
- When splitting large functions, centralize control flow in parent functions
- Move non-branching logic to helper functions
- Keep leaf functions pure when possible
- Centralize state manipulation in parent functions

## Guidelines for Function Splitting
- Aim for inverse hourglass shape: few parameters, simple return type, meaty logic
- Push control flow (if/switch statements) up to parent functions
- Push implementation details (for loops, calculations) down to helper functions
- Let parent functions manage state, helpers compute changes
- Each function should have a single, clear responsibility

## Examples

<example>
// Good: Small, focused function
function calculateTotal(items) {
  return items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price, 0);
}

// Good: Parent function centralizes control flow
function processUserData(userData) {
  if (!validateUserData(userData)) {
    return { success: false, error: "Invalid user data" };
  }
  
  const processedData = transformUserData(userData);
  const savedResult = saveToDatabase(processedData);
  
  return { success: true, result: savedResult };
}
</example>

<example type="invalid">
// Bad: Function too long (exceeds 70 lines)
function processEverything(data) {
  // Imagine 80+ lines of code here doing multiple things:
  // - data validation
  // - data transformation
  // - business logic
  // - error handling
  // - database interaction
  // - formatting response
  // All these concerns should be split into separate functions
}

// Bad: File with too many lines (exceeds 300)
// utilities.js - 350 lines of unrelated helper functions that
// should be split into separate modules by concern
</example> 